// =====================================================================================
// ATH-008 : Bulk data staged into an archive
// Severity: MEDIUM, raised to HIGH when both bulk source AND staging destination
// Pandas  : src/ath/hunting/rules/process_rules.py :: DataStagingArchive
//
// Before exfiltration, data is collected and compressed into one archive, usually
// written somewhere unremarkable and world-writable (C:\Windows\Temp, C:\Users\Public,
// ProgramData) rather than the operator's own profile.
//
// Requires TWO conditions -- an archive tool AND (bulk wildcard OR staging path).
// Firing on every Compress-Archive would be intolerable; a user zipping one file to
// email is normal behaviour.
//
// This detects COLLECTION, not exfiltration. Proving data left requires correlating an
// outbound transfer, which is a chaining problem, not a single-event rule.
// =====================================================================================

// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// REQUIRED TELEMETRY FIELDS (our synthetic schema)
//   process_events: timestamp, device, user, process_name, parent_process_name,
//            command_line, event_id
//
// SYNTHETIC SCHEMA vs MICROSOFT DEFENDER / SENTINEL
// DeviceProcessEvents mapping. What is missing on both sides here is FILE telemetry:
// Defender's DeviceFileEvents would show the archive being created and its size,
// which is how you would confirm bulk collection rather than inferring it from a
// command line. Our synthetic schema has no file-event table at all, so neither the
// Python rule nor this query can measure how much data was actually staged.
//
// LIKELY FALSE POSITIVES
//   - Legitimate backup jobs and scheduled archive tasks.
//   - Users compressing a folder to share or email.
//   - Log rotation, diagnostic bundles, software build/packaging steps.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

let LookBack = 7d;
DeviceProcessEvents
| where Timestamp > ago(LookBack)
| where ProcessCommandLine has_any (
    "Compress-Archive", "7z.exe", "7za.exe", "7zr.exe", "winrar.exe",
    "rar.exe", "tar.exe", "makecab.exe", "ZipFile")
| extend
    BulkSource  = ProcessCommandLine matches regex @"(?i)-path\s+\S*\*|\s\S+\\\*(\s|$)",
    StagingDest = ProcessCommandLine matches regex
        @"(?i)(c:\\windows\\temp|c:\\temp|c:\\users\\public|\\appdata\\local\\temp|c:\\programdata|\\\$recycle\.bin)"
| where BulkSource or StagingDest
| extend Severity = iff(BulkSource and StagingDest, "High", "Medium")
| project
    Timestamp, DeviceName, AccountName, FileName,
    BulkSource, StagingDest, Severity,
    InitiatingProcessFileName, ProcessCommandLine, ReportId
| sort by Timestamp asc

// Chaining idea for the next milestone: join this to DeviceNetworkEvents on DeviceName
// where an outbound external connection follows within ~30 minutes. Archive creation
// followed by egress is a far stronger signal than either alone.
//
// pandas equivalent:
//   if not _ARCHIVE_TOOLS.search(cmd): continue
//   staged, bulk = bool(_STAGING_PATHS.search(cmd)), bool(_BULK_SOURCE.search(cmd))
//   if not (staged or bulk): continue
